#{[17592186281267 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281267 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f48d194b-9d36-54e9-be97-ae912aef2d30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n\nII. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nIII. $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281284 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6d/6debac35386e085a0197cf9eb3f2da65cc719ace67163cad625f58c056fb8f67.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb88fa68-158a-5357-aaca-59045c1933c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281306 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b805c707981b9b6fcfa2ed387ad32a024fc845e6fd355a4b751ede4ad6a97e.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69708aa698c38f8a29d3eb69083f893fd8fca183bd713387456624fc38b86fe9.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379786 #edb/ref 17592